Some best practices for optimizing JavaScript code for performance include minimizing the number of DOM manipulations, using efficient data structures and algorithms, and minimizing the use of global variables.
To optimize the provided `fibonacci` function for better performance, we can implement memoization to avoid redundant calculations. Memoization is a technique where the function stores the results of expensive function calls and returns the cached result when the same inputs occur again.
Here is an example of how the `fibonacci` function can be refactored to use memoization:
```
const memo = {};
function fibonacci(n) {
  if (n < 2) {
    return n;
  } else if (memo[n]) {
    return memo[n];
  } else {
    memo[n] = fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
    return memo[n];
  }
}
console.log(fibonacci(10)); // Output: 55
```
In this refactored code, we use an object called `memo` to store the results of previous function calls. If the function is called with the same argument again, it returns the cached result from `memo` instead of recalculating it. This approach significantly reduces the number of function calls and improves the performance of the function.

