  const cubeState = [];
  const table = document.getElementById('cube-table');
  for (let i = 0; i < table.rows.length; i++) {
    const row = table.rows[i];
    const cubeRow = [];
    for (let j = 0; j < row.cells.length; j++) {
      const cell = row.cells[j];
      const color = cell.style.backgroundColor;
      // Use the color equivalence number instead of the color name
      cubeRow.push(COLOR_EQUIVALENCE[color] || COLOR_EQUIVALENCE['missing']);
    }
    cubeState.push(cubeRow);
  }
  return cubeState;
}
```
3. Modify the function that solves the cube to use the color equivalence numbers:
```javascript
function solveCube() {
  const cubeState = readCubeState();
  // Replace any missing colors with the missing color equivalence number
  const missingColor = COLOR_EQUIVALENCE['missing'];
  for (let i = 0; i < cubeState.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < cubeState[i].length; j++) {
      if (cubeState[i][j] === missingColor) {
        cubeState[i][j] = missingColor;
      }
    }
  }
  // Solve the cube using the modified cube state
  // ...
}
```
By using color equivalence numbers and treating missing or faded colors as a separate color, your algorithm can handle situations where some colors on the cube are missing or faded.
