Sure! Here's a program in Java that calculates and displays the current phase of the moon based on its illumination percentage:
```java
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit;
public class MoonPhaseCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate now = LocalDate.now();
        LocalDate newMoon = LocalDate.of(2021, 8, 8);
        long daysSinceNewMoon = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(newMoon, now);
        double illumination = getIllumination(daysSinceNewMoon);
        String phase = getMoonPhase(illumination);
        System.out.printf("The current phase of the moon is %s (%.2f%% illuminated)", phase, illumination);
    }
    private static double getIllumination(long daysSinceNewMoon) {
        return 100.0 * Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * daysSinceNewMoon / 29.53);
    }
    private static String getMoonPhase(double illumination) {
        if (illumination == 0) {
            return "New Moon";
        } else if (illumination < 25) {
            return "Waxing Crescent";
        } else if (illumination == 25) {
            return "First Quarter";
        } else if (illumination < 50) {
            return "Waxing Gibbous";
        } else if (illumination == 50) {
            return "Full Moon";
        } else if (illumination < 75) {
            return "Waning Gibbous";
        } else if (illumination == 75) {
            return "Last Quarter";
        } else {
            return "Waning Crescent";
        }
    }
}
```
This program calculates the number of days since the last new moon (which occurred on August 8, 2021), and then uses that value to calculate the moon's illumination percentage using a simple formula. It then uses that percentage to determine the current phase of the moon and displays it along with the illumination percentage.
Here's a brief explanation of the different phases of the moon:
- New Moon: The moon is not visible because it is in the same direction as the sun.
- Waxing Crescent: The moon is visible as a thin crescent in the western sky after sunset.
- First Quarter: The moon is half illuminated and visible in the southern sky during the afternoon and early evening.
- Waxing Gibbous: The moon is more than half illuminated and visible in the eastern sky during the late afternoon and evening.
- Full Moon: The moon is fully illuminated and visible in the eastern sky after sunset.
- Waning Gibbous: The moon is more than half illuminated and visible in the western sky during the late evening and early morning.
- Last Quarter: The moon is half illuminated and visible in the southern sky during the late night and early morning.
- Waning Crescent: The moon is visible as a thin crescent in the eastern sky just before sunrise.

