To implement a check for unique time signatures for each song in a curated musical compilation using Java code, you can follow these steps:
1. Create a Java class to represent a song, which will contain a field to store the time signature of the song.
2. Create a Java class to represent the musical compilation, which will contain a list of songs.
3. Implement a method in the musical compilation class that will check if each song in the list has a unique time signature. You can do this by iterating over the list of songs, and for each song, comparing its time signature to the time signatures of the other songs in the list.
4. If a song has a time signature that is the same as another song in the list, you can throw an exception or return a message indicating that the compilation does not have unique time signatures.
Here's some sample Java code to get you started:
```
public class Song {
  private String title;
  private String artist;
  private String timeSignature;
  public Song(String title, String artist, String timeSignature) {
    this.title = title;
    this.artist = artist;
    this.timeSignature = timeSignature;
  }
  public String getTimeSignature() {
    return timeSignature;
  }
}
public class MusicalCompilation {
  private List<Song> songs;
  public MusicalCompilation(List<Song> songs) {
    this.songs = songs;
  }
  public void checkUniqueTimeSignatures() throws Exception {
    for (int i = 0; i < songs.size(); i++) {
      String timeSignature = songs.get(i).getTimeSignature();
      for (int j = i + 1; j < songs.size(); j++) {
        if (timeSignature.equals(songs.get(j).getTimeSignature())) {
          throw new Exception("Compilation does not have unique time signatures");
        }
      }
    }
    System.out.println("Compilation has unique time signatures");
  }
}
```
In this example, the `Song` class has a `timeSignature` field and a `getTimeSignature()` method. The `MusicalCompilation` class has a `songs` field and a `checkUniqueTimeSignatures()` method that iterates over the list of songs and checks if each song has a unique time signature. If a non-unique time signature is found, an exception is thrown. Otherwise, a message is printed indicating that the compilation has unique time signatures.

