Sure, I can definitely help you with that! One possible solution to establish a persistent connection between the client and the server without using WebSocket or Socket.IO is to use the Server-Sent Events (SSE) protocol.
SSE is a simple and lightweight protocol that allows the server to send data to the client over a single HTTP connection. The client subscribes to the server's events by sending a GET request to a specific endpoint, and the server responds with a stream of data that the client can handle in real-time.
Here's a sample code to demonstrate how to handle incoming and outgoing messages using SSE in JavaScript:
On the client-side, you can create an EventSource object to connect to the server's SSE endpoint and handle incoming messages:
```
const source = new EventSource('/sse');
source.onmessage = function(event) {
  const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
  console.log(`Received message: ${data.message}`);
};
source.onerror = function(event) {
  console.error('Error occurred:', event);
};
```
On the server-side, you can create a route that sends SSE events to the subscribed clients:
```
app.get('/sse', function(req, res) {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');
  // Send a welcome message to the client
  res.write('data: {"message": "Welcome to the chat!"}\n\n');
  // Handle incoming messages from the client
  req.on('data', function(data) {
    const message = JSON.parse(data);
    console.log(`Received message: ${message.message}`);
    // Handle the message and send a response back to the client
    res.write(`data: {"message": "You said: ${message.message}"}\n\n`);
  });
  // Handle client disconnection
  req.on('close', function() {
    console.log('Client disconnected');
    res.end();
  });
});
```
This code sets the necessary headers for SSE, sends a welcome message to the client, and handles incoming messages from the client. When a client sends a message, the server logs the message and sends a response back to the client. Finally, when the client disconnects, the server logs the disconnection and ends the SSE stream.
