Can you provide an example of a C++ function that demonstrates the proverb "A stitch in time saves nine"?
Consider the following C++ code that performs a simple task of sorting an array:
void sortArray(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        for (int j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[j]) {
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
However, we can optimize this code by adding a check to see if the array is already sorted before performing the sorting algorithm. This small change can save us a lot of time and resources in the long run.
Can you modify the code to demonstrate the proverb "A stitch in time saves nine" by adding this optimization?

