void sortArray(int arr[], int n) {
    bool sorted = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) {
            sorted = false;
            break;
        }
    }
    if (!sorted) {
        for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
            for (int j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
                if (arr[i] > arr[j]) {
                    int temp = arr[i];
                    arr[i] = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
This code first checks if the array is already sorted by iterating through the array and comparing adjacent elements. If it's already sorted, the function simply returns without performing any sorting. If it's not sorted, it performs the sorting algorithm as before. This small optimization can save a lot of time and resources in the long run, especially for large arrays that are already sorted.
